# You may not use this file except in compliance with the License.
# You may obtain a copy of the License at
#
#    http://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0
#
# Unless required by applicable law or agreed to in writing,
# software distributed under the License is distributed on an
# "AS IS" BASIS, WITHOUT WARRANTIES OR CONDITIONS OF ANY KIND,
# either express or implied. See the License for the specific
# language governing permissions and limitations under the License
# ******************************************************************
#
# `docker build` uploads a whole context to the daemon before the first
# instruction runs, and .git history plus Maven output dominate that
# tree while no image layer can use either. Excluding them is not only
# faster but DETERMINISTIC: a context carrying target/ makes the result
# depend on whatever a previous local `mvn` run left behind.
#
# The rules here are deliberately NOT copied from .gitignore, because
# the two syntaxes look identical and behave differently. Every pattern
# is ANCHORED AT THE CONTEXT ROOT: a bare `*.log` matches ./x.log and
# nothing else, and depth-independent matching needs an explicit `**/`
# prefix. That is why the metadata, editor and credential rules below
# all carry one and the build-output rules deliberately do not - `target`
# unprefixed reaches ONLY the root build directory, leaving any path
# component of that name under src/test/resources in the context, where
# parity baselines may live. `**/target` would silently strip protected
# fixture space. pom.xml declares no <modules>, so the anchored form is
# complete as well as safe; a multi-module build would need one anchored
# rule per module rather than a `**/` prefix. Docker also always sends
# Dockerfile and .dockerignore themselves, so neither has a rule.
#
# The LAST matching pattern wins, so a `!` negation must follow the rule
# it re-includes. There is exactly one negation here and it is live:
# `.env.*` genuinely matches .env.example, which holds variable names
# and empty placeholders only and must stay available.
#
# No glob below matches a content format this repository ships: there is
# deliberately no *.txt, *.md, *.json, *.sql, *.yml, *.html or *.zip
# rule, and no bare `app`, `build` or `data` rule either, because the
# fixed-width parity fixtures and the VSAM catalogue listing are .txt,
# application and dashboard resources are .json, and `data` is a
# real path component of app/data. A broad rule that looks harmless today
# hides the next deliverable on the day it arrives, silently and with no
# error. The extension globs that do appear cover only build output,
# runtime files, private key material and editor state.
#
# Four things are deliberately NOT excluded. diagrams/, .github/,
# .gitignore and .gitattributes, because all four are tracked shared
# content rather than local artefacts, and .gitattributes in particular
# records which corpus members are CRLF-terminated, which a fixed-width
# parity check may need. Build reports, because pom.xml pins the JaCoCo
# and OWASP output under ${project.build.directory} and Surefire and
# Failsafe default there, so `target` already covers them. And a wrapper
# jar, because .mvn/wrapper/maven-wrapper.properties sets
# distributionType only-script and none is ever fetched.
#
# HOW TO VERIFY A CHANGE TO THIS FILE
#   Ask Docker rather than reasoning about the patterns; the anchoring
#   differences above are too easy to get wrong by eye.
#
#     printf 'FROM alpine:3.17\nCOPY . /ctx\nRUN find /ctx -type f\n' \
#       > /tmp/ctx.Dockerfile
#     DOCKER_BUILDKIT=0 docker build --no-cache -f /tmp/ctx.Dockerfile .
#
#   The listing it prints IS the context. app/ (less EBCDIC), src/,
#   pom.xml, mvnw, mvnw.cmd, .mvn/, Dockerfile, docker-compose.yml,
#   localstack-init/, observability/, docs/ and .env.example must all
#   appear; .git, target, samples and app/data/EBCDIC must not. Then
#   confirm the real build still resolves every COPY:
#
#     docker build --no-cache -t carddemo:verify .
#
# Never add a glob for a format this repository ships, never add a bare
# `app`, and never convert an anchored build-output rule to a `**/` form.
# Each failure is SILENT - docker build prints nothing, the file simply
# is not in the context, and the first symptom is a fixture-not-found
# test failure or a gate that cannot be evidenced, attributed to the
# test rather than to this file. The context listing above is the only
# check that catches this class of mistake.

# Packfiles no image layer can use. Safe to drop because no plugin in
# this build reads git metadata: there is no git-commit-id or buildnumber
# plugin deriving a version or build stamp.
.git

# Maven build output. Anchored on purpose, for the reason given above,
# and dropping it also keeps a stale local jar from masking a fresh
# in-image build.
target

# IntelliJ IDEA's own output, written to the project root when the IDE
# builds in place instead of delegating to Maven.
out

# A stray class file left beside a source by an ad-hoc `javac`. It lands
# outside target/, so the rule above does not cover it, and it can land
# at any depth in the source tree.
**/*.class

# JVM fatal-error, replay and heap-dump files. The working directory of a
# forked Surefire JVM is not necessarily the project root, so these are
# depth-independent.
**/hs_err_pid*
**/replay_pid*
**/*.hprof

# Local runtime output from a developer's own run. The application logs
# to stdout in a container, so anything matching here is host-local. The
# logs directory is anchored: a nested one belongs to whatever produced
# it and the wildcard rules cover it instead.
logs
**/*.log
**/*.pid
**/*.tmp

# Local environment overrides. `.env.*` also matches the template
# .env.example, so the negation that follows is required rather than
# defensive - it is the only negation in this file. The JWT signing key
# is environment-indirected with no committed default, so real values
# live only in .env and must never reach the daemon or a layer.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Private key material and credential stores. These are preventive
# controls: nothing matches one today, and that is the point - the value
# is firing before the accident. Public certificate formats are
# deliberately absent, so a public test certificate added later stays
# available; id_rsa and id_ed25519 are matched exactly, not as id_rsa*,
# so a .pub half is not caught too. All are depth-independent, because a
# leaked credential is a hazard wherever it lands and no deliverable
# carries any of these names.
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.p12
**/*.pfx
**/*.jks
**/*.keystore
**/id_rsa
**/id_ed25519
**/.aws
**/credentials
**/secrets

# IDE and editor state, all of it user-local. Unlike .gitignore, which
# keeps the three shareable .vscode team files, the whole directory goes
# here: none of them is a build input, so there is nothing to rescue.
**/.idea
**/*.iml
**/*.ipr
**/*.iws
**/.vscode
**/.classpath
**/.project
**/.settings
**/.factorypath
**/.apt_generated

# Editor swap and backup files, and the residue `git merge` and
# `git apply` leave on a conflict: a .orig or .rej would otherwise be
# copied into a layer beside the file it shadows.
**/*.swp
**/*.swo
**/*~
**/*.bak
**/*.orig
**/*.rej

# Operating-system metadata, macOS then Windows. Also preventive: a file
# browser creates these, not this project, and they appear at arbitrary
# depth when they appear at all.
**/.DS_Store
**/._*
**/.Spotlight-V100
**/.Trashes
**/Thumbs.db
**/desktop.ini

# Platform scratch: screenshots and recordings, and the ad-hoc validation
# scripts that must never reach a commit, let alone an image layer.
blitzy
**/blitzy_adhoc_test_*

# The two content trees below are the single point where this file
# diverges from .gitignore, which keeps both. The two answer different
# questions: .gitignore decides what reaches a CLONE, where these must
# survive byte-for-byte as the reference of record, while this file
# decides what reaches the BUILD DAEMON, and the Java build never parses
# either of them. Directory form is used rather than a trailing `/**` so
# the daemon prunes each subtree without descending into it.

# z/OS build tooling: three compile templates, three build procedures and
# two binary emulator runtime archives under samples/m2 that account for
# nearly the whole tree. Nothing here has a Java analogue; pom.xml
# supersedes the set. Excluding the directory is also what makes a
# `*.zip` rule unnecessary - and such a rule is exactly the kind that
# would later hide those archives from a clone if copied across.
samples

# The EBCDIC datasets: fixed-width .PS files retained as byte-level
# codepage reference only, never transcoded and never read by the build.
# The ASCII fixtures under app/data/ASCII are the authoritative seed and
# test input, which is why this names the EBCDIC directory specifically
# and never app/data, which would take both.
app/data/EBCDIC

# Everything else is protected by OMISSION, not by negation: app/ (less
# the EBCDIC datasets), src/, docs/, diagrams/, observability/,
# localstack-init/, .github/, .mvn/, pom.xml, mvnw, mvnw.cmd, Dockerfile,
# docker-compose.yml and the evidence artefacts all stay in the context
# because no rule above reaches them. A line such as `!app/**` would read
# as protection while doing nothing at all, since a negation only takes
# effect against an earlier pattern that matches. The guarantee is
# asserted by the context listing above instead.
#
# One case is worth stating because a wildcard makes it easy to lose:
# app/jcl holds 29 members, 28 ending in a lowercase .jcl and
# CREASTMT.JCL - the sole source for statement generation - ending in an
# uppercase .JCL. Docker matches case-sensitively, so any pattern written
# as app/jcl/*.jcl would treat the two differently. No rule here touches
# app/jcl at all, which is the only form of that guarantee that cannot be
# got wrong.
